const ABSTRACT =
  'A Langevin sampler discretizes a diffusion whose invariant law is the target, and dropping the ' +
  'Metropolis step (ULA) leaves a discretization bias. On a Gaussian target that bias has a closed ' +
  'form, so the simulations here are graded against the exact answer rather than against a longer ' +
  'run of themselves: in all 30 cases, ULA’s measured variance lies within three standard errors of ' +
  'the exact law, whose excess over the target is first order in the step size. Keeping the ' +
  'Metropolis step (MALA) returns the target variances at the price of rejected proposals, but on a ' +
  'separated two-well mixture its crossing rate still falls roughly exponentially with the barrier, ' +
  'as ULA’s does. Live figures check the dynamics too, against the classical Lifson–Jackson effective ' +
  'diffusivity. The project is advised by Dr. David Herzog; a manuscript with him on transport-coefficient ' +
  'estimation for molecular systems is in preparation, and its results are held back pending submission.';

