  <!-- Overview -->
  <Container first id="overview">
    <h1>Overview</h1>
    <p class="text">
      This project is carried out with Dr. David Herzog, Department of Mathematics, Iowa State
      University, who advises it. It has a public part and an unpublished one. The public part, the
      <a href={REPO}>langevin-samplers</a> repository and this site’s live figures, is reported here
      with its results. The unpublished part is a manuscript in preparation with Dr. Herzog on
      transport-coefficient estimation for molecular systems; <a href="#molecular-systems">its
      section</a> says what it is about, and its results are held back pending submission.
    </p>
    <p class="text">
      Stochastic-gradient Langevin dynamics, the inner loop of annealed Langevin sampling and the
      corrector step of a predictor–corrector diffusion sampler all run the same Euler–Maruyama
      update, and none of them performs the accept/reject test that would make it exact in
      stationarity. That mapping is this site’s framing; the repository behind this page tests only
      the two textbook samplers, the unadjusted Langevin algorithm (ULA) and its Metropolis-adjusted
      version (MALA). It asks what skipping the test costs, in a setting where the cost has a closed
      form.
    </p>
    <p class="text">
      On a Gaussian target the unadjusted chain’s invariant law is known exactly, so the simulation
      is graded against the answer rather than against a longer run of itself, and a failure would
      convict the sampler rather than the theory. The same study then shows the limit of the fix. On
      a separated two-well target, the Metropolis-corrected sampler’s crossing rate falls with the
      barrier much as the uncorrected one’s does: correcting discretization bias does not by itself
      ensure rapid mixing.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrapper">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>What the saved runs and checks show</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Does ULA’s measured variance agree with the exact law <Tex src={String.raw`v(h)`} />?</td><td>In all <b>30</b> coordinate–step-size cases, within three estimated standard errors (relative SEs 0.11% to 0.85%)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>How large is the bias near the stability limit, <Tex src={String.raw`h = 0.4`} /> with <Tex src={String.raw`h^* = 0.5`} />?</td><td>Measured [1.2479, 1.2488, 4.2085] against exact [1.25, 1.25, 4.2105], for targets [0.25, 1, 4]</td></tr>
          <tr><td>What does the Metropolis step buy, and at what price?</td><td>MALA returns [0.2494, 1.0003, 4.0075]; its acceptance falls from 0.999 to 0.595 over the sweep</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Does it fix slow mixing between modes?</td><td>Not here. Log crossing rate against the barrier: slope <b>−0.948</b> (ULA, fitted on <Tex src={String.raw`\mu \le 4.5`} />) and <b>−1.032</b> (MALA, fitted on <Tex src={String.raw`\mu \le 4.0`} />), near the leading exponential’s −1</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Are the dynamics right, not only the invariant law?</td><td>The site’s check script (not the repository) measures <Tex src={String.raw`\hat D(2000) = 0.6218`} /> (s.e. 0.0098) against the Lifson–Jackson value 0.62386</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text">
      The sections below set up the update, derive the exact reference and run it live, show what
      the correction costs and what it does not fix, and then turn from the invariant law to the
      dynamics, before describing the molecular-systems manuscript.
    </p>
  </Container>

