    <h1>One update, many names</h1>
    <p class="text">
      Let <Tex src={String.raw`\pi`} /> be a target density on <Tex src={String.raw`\mathbb{R}^d`} />
      with score <Tex src={String.raw`\nabla\log\pi`} />. The overdamped Langevin diffusion
    </p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`dX_t = \nabla \log \pi(X_t)\,dt + \sqrt{2}\,dW_t`} />
    <p class="text">
      leaves <Tex src={String.raw`\pi`} /> invariant, so discretizing it gives a sampler. With step
      size <Tex src={String.raw`h>0`} /> and <Tex src={String.raw`\xi_k \sim \mathcal N(0, I)`} />
      i.i.d., its Euler–Maruyama discretization is ULA,
    </p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`X_{k+1} = X_k + h\,\nabla \log \pi(X_k) + \sqrt{2h}\,\xi_k ,`} />
    <p class="text">
      whose invariant law <Tex src={String.raw`\pi_h`} /> generally differs from
      <Tex src={String.raw`\pi`} />. That gap is the discretization bias. MALA uses the same move as
      a proposal <Tex src={String.raw`y`} /> and accepts it with probability
    </p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`\alpha(x,y) = \min\!\Big(1, \frac{\pi(y)\,q(x\mid y)}{\pi(x)\,q(y\mid x)}\Big), \qquad q(y\mid x) = \mathcal N\big(y;\ x + h\nabla\log\pi(x),\ 2hI\big),`} />
    <p class="text">
      which restores exact reversibility with respect to <Tex src={String.raw`\pi`} />. In
      stationarity MALA is unbiased at every step size, so its only error is Monte Carlo; what it
      pays instead is rejected proposals.
    </p>
  </Container>

