  <!-- Exact reference -->
  <Container id="exact">
    <h1>An exact reference for the bias</h1>
    <p class="text">
      Take <Tex src={String.raw`\pi = \mathcal N(0, \operatorname{diag}(s^2))`} />, with coordinate
      scales <Tex src={String.raw`s_i`} />. ULA then acts coordinatewise as an AR(1) recursion,
    </p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`X' = \Big(1 - \frac{h}{s^2}\Big)X + \sqrt{2h}\,\xi, \qquad \text{stationary iff } 0 < h < 2s^2 .`} />
    <p class="text">
      Write <Tex src={String.raw`a = 1 - h/s^2`} />. The stationary variance solves
      <Tex src={String.raw`v = a^2 v + 2h`} />, and since
      <Tex src={String.raw`1 - a^2 = (h/s^2)(2 - h/s^2)`} />,
    </p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`v(h) = \frac{2h}{1-a^2} = \frac{s^2}{1 - h/(2s^2)}, \qquad v(h) - s^2 = \frac{h/2}{1 - h/(2s^2)} = \frac{h}{2} + O(h^2).`} />
  </Container>

  <NoteRow>
    <p class="text">
      So ULA over-disperses at every step size, by an amount first order in
      <Tex src={String.raw`h`} />. Diagonal Gaussians couple coordinatewise,
      <Tex src={String.raw`W_2^2(\pi_h, \pi) = \sum_i (\sqrt{v_i} - s_i)^2`} />, and
      <Tex src={String.raw`\sqrt{v_i} - s_i \approx h/(4s_i)`} />, so the induced 2-Wasserstein error
      is first order too:
    </p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`W_2(\pi_h, \pi) = \frac{h}{4}\Big(\sum_i s_i^{-2}\Big)^{1/2} + O(h^2).`} />
    <p class="text">
      Because <Tex src={String.raw`\pi_h`} /> is known exactly, the measured ULA variance has to agree
      with <Tex src={String.raw`v(h)`} /> to within Monte Carlo error.
    </p>
    <Fragment slot="note">
      To leading order the inflation is <Tex src={String.raw`h/2`} /> whatever the scale
      <Tex src={String.raw`s`} />. That is exact theory, not a measurement, and it is only the leading
      term: at <Tex src={String.raw`h = 0.4`} />, <Tex src={String.raw`s = 0.5`} /> the exact
      inflation is 1.0, against <Tex src={String.raw`h/2 = 0.2`} />.
    </Fragment>
  </NoteRow>

  <Container>
    <p class="text">
      The Gaussian experiment runs both samplers on scales <Tex src={String.raw`s = (0.5, 1, 2)`} />,
      a 4:1 scale ratio and 16:1 covariance conditioning, at 10 log-spaced step sizes from 0.005 to
      0.4, below the stability limit <Tex src={String.raw`h^* = 2\min_i s_i^2 = 0.5`} />. Each
      configuration runs 128 chains for 200,000 steps from an overdispersed start (a standard normal
      times 2), keeping every 20th draw after a 20% burn-in. Variance standard errors come from
      independent-chain averages of <Tex src={String.raw`(X - \bar X)^2`} />, and the source is
      explicit about what they cover: “It measures sampling error, not common initialization bias or
      a failure to reach stationarity.”
    </p>
  </Container>

