      <b>Figure 2.</b> Gaussian stationary variance against the exact ULA law and target variance
      (the repository’s Figure 1). One panel per scale, <Tex src={String.raw`s = 0.5, 1, 2`} />: the
      measured stationary variance against <Tex src={String.raw`h`} /> for ULA (filled circles) and
      MALA (open squares), the exact <Tex src={String.raw`v(h) = s^2/(1-h/2s^2)`} />, the target
      <Tex src={String.raw`s^2`} /> (dashed) and the stability limit
      <Tex src={String.raw`h^* = 0.5`} /> (dotted). Anisotropic Gaussian, 128 chains × 200,000 steps
      per configuration. All 30 ULA cases lie within three estimated standard errors of
      <Tex src={String.raw`v(h)`} />.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <Container>
    <p class="text">
      In the saved run, all 30 coordinate–step-size cases lie within three estimated variance
      standard errors of <Tex src={String.raw`v(h)`} />, with relative standard errors from 0.11% to
      0.85%. Near the limit the bias is large, and it is still exactly where the formula puts it:
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrapper">
      <table>
        <thead class="center">
          <tr><th>scale <Tex src={String.raw`s`} /></th><th>target <Tex src={String.raw`s^2`} /></th><th>ULA exact <Tex src={String.raw`v(h)`} /></th><th>ULA measured</th><th>MALA measured</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="center">
          {T1.map((t) => <tr><td>{t.s}</td><td>{t.s2}</td><td>{t.exact}</td><td>{t.ula}</td><td>{t.mala}</td></tr>)}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text">
      All at <Tex src={String.raw`h = 0.4`} />, where MALA’s acceptance rate is 0.595.
    </p>
    <h2>Run it</h2>
    <p class="text">
      The widget below runs one coordinate of that experiment in your browser. ULA acts
      coordinatewise, so a single coordinate is exactly one coordinate of the repository’s run. It
      moves 4,096 independent chains from a fixed seed, draws their histogram against
      <Tex src={String.raw`\mathcal N(0, s^2)`} /> (dashed) and
      <Tex src={String.raw`\mathcal N(0, v(h))`} /> (solid), and traces the ensemble variance against
      the step <Tex src={String.raw`k`} />. For ULA the reference is exact at every step, not only in
      the limit. Iterating <Tex src={String.raw`v_{k+1} = a^2 v_k + 2h`} /> from
      <Tex src={String.raw`\operatorname{Var}(X_0)`} /> and subtracting the fixed point gives
    </p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`\operatorname{Var}(X_k) = v(h) + a^{2k}\big(\operatorname{Var}(X_0) - v(h)\big), \qquad a = 1 - h/s^2,`} />
    <p class="text">
      an AR(1) identity derived from the update (it is not in the repository). Push
      <Tex src={String.raw`h`} /> past <Tex src={String.raw`2s^2`} /> and the formula has no
      stationary law to offer; the widget says so from the formula rather than waiting for the
      chains to overflow.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <Container width="extra-large" tone="gray" id="baseline">
    <LangevinGaussianBaseline />
    <p class="caption">
      <b>Figure 3.</b> Classical baseline, run live: ULA and MALA on a one-dimensional Gaussian
      <Tex src={String.raw`\mathcal N(0, s^2)`} />, with the repository’s update, closed form and
      acceptance rule, from a fixed seed. The readout compares the ensemble variance with the exact
      <Tex src={String.raw`\operatorname{Var}(X_k)`} /> (ULA) or with <Tex src={String.raw`s^2`} />
      (MALA, valid in stationarity), in units of its independent-chain standard error. MALA’s
      acceptance rate here has no closed-form reference, and it need not match the repository’s
      0.595 at <Tex src={String.raw`h = 0.4`} />: that figure is for the three-coordinate target,
      where MALA accepts or rejects all coordinates jointly.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <!-- Conditioning -->
  <Container id="conditioning">
    <h1>The narrow direction sets the ceiling</h1>
    <p class="text">
      Within one target, the coordinates disagree about the step size. The step-size ceiling comes
      from the narrowest direction, <Tex src={String.raw`h < 2\min_i s_i^2`} />, while the time to
      decorrelate comes from the widest, which is why ill-conditioned targets are hard for Langevin
      methods. On the repository’s target this shows up directly in the AR(1) coefficients.
    </p>
  </Container>

