  <Container>
    <p class="text">
      At <Tex src={String.raw`h = 0.4`} />, ULA’s slowest-coordinate ESS per gradient is 0.0308 and
      MALA’s is 0.0225. Read next to Figure 2, ULA’s edge is in effective draws from
      <Tex src={String.raw`\pi_h`} />, not from <Tex src={String.raw`\pi`} />. In the repository’s
      words, “ULA’s error is a bias you cannot average away; MALA’s is a cost paid in rejected
      proposals.” The cost, across the sweep:
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrapper">
      <table>
        <thead class="center"><tr><th><Tex src={String.raw`h`} /></th>{T2.map(([hv]) => <th>{hv}</th>)}</tr></thead>
        <tbody class="center"><tr><td>MALA acceptance</td>{T2.map(([, acc]) => <td>{acc}</td>)}</tr></tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text">
      Rounded to four decimals from the saved values; the README rounds the end points to 0.999 and
      0.595.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <!-- Metastability -->
  <Container id="mixing">
    <h1>What the correction does not fix</h1>
    <p class="text">
      The second experiment isolates the opposite failure. The target is an equal mixture of two
      Gaussians separated along the first coordinate <Tex src={String.raw`e_0`} />,
    </p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`\pi = \tfrac12\,\mathcal N(-\mu e_0, s^2 I) + \tfrac12\,\mathcal N(+\mu e_0, s^2 I), \qquad \Delta(\mu) = \frac{\mu^2}{2s^2} - \log 2 ,`} />
    <p class="text">
      where <Tex src={String.raw`\Delta`} /> is a large-separation approximation to the barrier (the
      exact modes sit slightly closer to zero). Both samplers equilibrate rapidly within a mode and
      cross between modes on an exponentially longer timescale. Kramers-type asymptotics suggest a
      leading exponential <Tex src={String.raw`e^{-\Delta}`} />, with a separation-dependent
      prefactor.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <NoteRow>
    <p class="text">
      The run uses one step size, <Tex src={String.raw`h = 0.05`} />, in two dimensions with
      <Tex src={String.raw`s = 1`} /> and <Tex src={String.raw`\mu`} /> from 2.0 to 5.0: 32 chains of
      150,000 steps per separation, every chain started in the left well, sign crossings counted
      after a 20% burn-in (crossings during burn-in, or between steps, are not measured). The fit is
      <Tex src={String.raw`\log(\text{rate}) = c - b\,\Delta`} />, compared with
      <Tex src={String.raw`b \approx 1`} />, on separations with at least 20 crossings. That cutoff
      fits the two samplers on different supports: ULA through <Tex src={String.raw`\mu = 4.5`} /> (6
      points), MALA through <Tex src={String.raw`\mu = 4.0`} /> (5 points).
    </p>
    <Fragment slot="note">
      The barrier law is asymptotic, so the fitted slope is a numerical comparison, not an exact
      transition-rate identity. Unlike the Gaussian experiment, there is no closed form here to grade
      against.
    </Fragment>
  </NoteRow>

  <Container width="extra-large" tone="gray" id="fig-failure-modes">
    <svg class="diagram" viewBox="0 0 1100 480" role="img" aria-label="Diagram in two panels. Left, Bias: a dashed bell curve for the target N(0, s^2) and a slightly wider solid bell for ULA's invariant law N(0, v(h)), drawn at s = 1 and h = 0.4 where v(h) = 1.25. The excess v(h) - s^2 = h/2 + O(h^2) is first order in h, and MALA removes it in stationarity. Right, Mixing: the curve of minus log pi along the line through both modes of the two-component mixture, drawn exactly at mu = 3 and s = 1, with two wells at plus and minus mu and a saddle at zero. A bracket marks the barrier Delta, approximately mu^2/2s^2 - log 2, and an arrow over the saddle is labelled crossing rate of order exp(-Delta). MALA does not remove it. Fitted slopes -0.948 for ULA on mu up to 4.5 and -1.032 for MALA on mu up to 4.0, against -1, at one step size h = 0.05.">
