          {T4.map((t) => <tr><td>{t.mu}</td><td>{t.d}</td><td>{t.uc}</td><td>{t.un}</td><td>{t.mc}</td><td>{t.mn}</td></tr>)}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text">
      At <Tex src={String.raw`\mu = 5`} /> (<Tex src={String.raw`\Delta = 11.8`} />) neither sampler
      crosses during the measured window, and all 32 chains of each stay in the well they started
      in. A zero count bounds a rate rather than measuring it, so that row is a detection limit, not
      a further point on the line. Nor does MALA’s guarantee reach this regime: it is unbiased in
      stationarity, but a chain trapped near its initial well is nowhere near stationarity. One
      difference the repository leaves open: at <Tex src={String.raw`\mu = 4.5`} /> ULA records 27
      crossings to MALA’s 4, and the study does not test why.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <!-- Dynamics -->
  <Container id="dynamics">
    <h1>Dynamics, not only the invariant law</h1>
    <p class="text">
      A sampler can get the invariant measure right and still get the dynamics wrong. For a
      one-dimensional periodic potential the long-time diffusivity has an exact value, given by the
      classical Lifson–Jackson formula (a textbook benchmark, not a result of this project) with
      free diffusivity <Tex src={String.raw`D_0 = \beta^{-1}`} /> and
      averages over one period:
    </p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`D_{\mathrm{eff}} = \frac{D_0}{\langle e^{\beta V}\rangle\,\langle e^{-\beta V}\rangle}, \qquad V(x) = \cos x,\ \beta = 1:\quad D_{\mathrm{eff}} = I_0(1)^{-2} = 0.6238603604\ldots`} />
    <p class="text">
      Any Langevin integrator has to reproduce it as the step size shrinks. As this site puts it,
      “One that gets the invariant measure right and the diffusivity wrong has a bug in its time
      discretization.” Figure 9 runs
      the textbook mean-squared-displacement estimator live, with 1024 replicas at
      <Tex src={String.raw`\beta = 1`} /> and <Tex src={String.raw`h = 0.01`} />. The site’s check
      script, run at longer time, measures <Tex src={String.raw`\hat D(2000) = 0.6218`} /> with a
      standard error of 0.0098.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <Container width="large" tone="gray" id="diffusivity">
    <DiffusivityFigure n={9} />
  </Container>

  <Container>
    <p class="text">
      The band in Figure 10 is the same dynamics in two dimensions,
      <Tex src={String.raw`dX_t = -\nabla V(X_t)\,dt + \sqrt{2\beta^{-1}}\,dW_t`} /> with
      <Tex src={String.raw`V(x,y) = \cos x + \cos y`} /> on the torus, <Tex src={String.raw`\beta = 2`} />
      and <Tex src={String.raw`h = 0.004`} />. It is checked against its Gibbs law through
      <Tex src={String.raw`\mathbb E_\pi[\cos X] = -I_1(\beta)/I_0(\beta)`} />. The site’s check
      script recorded total-variation distances <Tex src={String.raw`\mathrm{TV}_{1D} = 1.021\times10^{-3}`} />
      and <Tex src={String.raw`\mathrm{TV}_{2D} = 3.945\times10^{-3}`} /> (measured 2026-07-30), and a
      discretization bias in <Tex src={String.raw`\mathbb E[\cos X]`} /> at the shipped step of
      <Tex src={String.raw`9\times10^{-4} \pm 4\times10^{-4}`} /> (one standard error), barely resolved
      from zero.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <Container width="extra-extra-large" tone="gray" id="live">
    <HeroSim priority={false} />
    <p class="caption">
      <b>Figure 10.</b> Overdamped Langevin dynamics in <Tex src={String.raw`V(x,y) = \cos x + \cos y`} />
      at <Tex src={String.raw`\beta = 2`} />, Euler–Maruyama at <Tex src={String.raw`h = 0.004`} />,
      running live in your browser. Particles collect in the potential wells, where the Gibbs measure
      <Tex src={String.raw`\propto e^{-\beta V}`} /> concentrates, and occasionally hop between
      neighboring wells; color encodes the local potential energy.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <Container>
    <p class="text">
      The third live figure is a related sampler from generative modeling. It runs a diffusion model’s
      reverse-time sampler with the exact score of a Gaussian mixture, so it can be graded on mode
      coverage rather than on how plausible its samples look; its caption gives the setup and the
      grade. It is the reverse-time update, not the Langevin corrector of Figure 1, and like Figures 9
      and 10 it lives in this site’s code, not in langevin-samplers.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <Container width="large" tone="gray" id="diffusion">
    <DiffusionSampler n={11} />
  </Container>

  <!-- Molecular systems -->
  <Container id="molecular-systems">
    <h1>What we are doing: molecular systems</h1>
    <p class="text">
      With Dr. David Herzog, Department of Mathematics, Iowa State University, we are preparing a
      manuscript, <em>Langevin Dynamics and Transport-Coefficient Estimation for Molecular
      Systems</em>. The work develops samplers for overdamped Langevin
      dynamics “staged from a 1D periodic potential up to a solvated ion in a 3D Lennard-Jones
      bath,” and uses them to estimate transport coefficients: the long-time constants, such as a
      diffusion coefficient, that describe how particles move rather than where they sit at
      equilibrium.
    </p>
    <p class="text">
      Why it matters is the point of the dynamics section above. A sampler can reproduce the
      invariant law and still get the dynamics wrong, and a transport coefficient is a property of
      the dynamics, so an equilibrium check alone does not test it. The sections above draw only on the
      public repository, this site’s live figures and textbook results; this page shows none of the
      manuscript’s methods or results.
    </p>
    <h2>Status</h2>
    <p class="text">
      In preparation. The manuscript’s methods and results are held back pending submission.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <!-- Reproduce -->
  <Container id="reproduce">
    <h1>Reproduce</h1>
    <p class="text">
      The repository figures and the saved results behind every table on this page come from
      <a href={REPO}>langevin-samplers</a> (MIT). Its README gives these commands:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={REPRODUCE} />
    <p class="text">
      Runs are seeded, with streams keyed by role and configuration, and each writes a sidecar that
      records its seed, parameters and commit. GitHub Actions runs the regression suite and a small
      end-to-end pipeline on every push and pull request, and the AR(1) uncertainty test compares
      predicted variance standard errors with the actual spread across independent simulated
      experiments.
    </p>
